  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose a multi-stage optimization approach that uses coarse-to-fine resolution and adaptive basis functions to find better solutions. This method alternates between optimizing a lower-dimensional representation of the function and refining it at higher resolutions with more detailed representations. The key innovation is using an adaptive basis representation that automatically handles the periodic structure of the optimal function.\n\nBy doing this:\n1. We reduce the dimensionality of the problem in coarse stages\n2. We preserve the essential properties of the solution when moving to finer stages\n3. We maintain stability and diversity across stages to avoid local optima\n4. We combine several local optimization techniques in each stage\n5. We systematically improve solutions through resolution transitions\n\nThis differs from existing methods by being multi-stage resolution-aware and by using basis-based compressive representations of the solution.\n\nMathematical specification:\nRepresentation:\n- We'll represent the solution at a given resolution as a linear combination of fixed basis functions:\n  h(x) = sum_{j=1}^{J} a_j \u03c6_j(x)\n\nwhere J << n (basis size) is the effective dimension.\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 a_j \u2264 1 for all j (basis coefficients)\n- \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1 (one linear constraint)\n\nObjective function (discrete version):\nC5 = max_k (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nWhere dx = 2.0/n\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Global setup\n   - Use provided initial_h_values if available\n   - Set resolutions from 64 to 512 (powers of 2)\n   - Initialize best solution as initial_h_values (512)\n   - Set time budget: 250s per stage, 100s for final 512 optimization\n\n2. Stage 1 (Low-resolution optimization)\n   - Evaluate existing best solution for 64-point representation\n   - Create basis \u03c6_j (simpleTriangleBasis for periodicity)\n   - Convert current solution to basis representation\n   - Initialize sparse solution (J=8 basis functions)\n   - Perform adaptive basis optimization:\n        * Local search with guided hill climbing\n        * SLSQP refinement (beta=20)\n        * Genetic algorithm with large population\n   - Transition to next resolution while preserving solution structure\n\n3. Stage 2 (Medium-resolution optimization)\n   - Similar to Stage 1 but with increased basis size (J=16)\n   - Perform cross-validation between resolved and un-resolved solutions\n   - Use quadratic regularization during basis optimization\n   - Augment basis functions automatically\n\n4. Stage 3 (High-resolution optimization)\n   - Same as previous stages but with full n=512 resolution\n   - Minimal SLSQP beta (beta=5) to preserve diversity\n   - Final gradient refinement (step=0.001)\n   - Time-limited hill climbing (max_iter=500)\n\n5. Solution handling\n   - After each stage, add new resolution\n   - Create upscaled solution from best candidate\n   - Maintain parent solution as fallback\n   - Evaluate final solution and enforce constraints\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Basis function selection: Auto-adaptive basis with fixed width\n- Optimization methods: Local search, global search, and constraints handling\n- Budget allocation:\n    - Low resolution: 250s (single resolution)\n    - Medium resolution: 250s\n    - High resolution: 350s + final optimization\n- Basis size progression:\n    - Start: J=8\n    - Medium: J=16\n    - High: J=32\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Estimated cost: ~800 seconds MATLAB implementation suggests <1100s\n- Deadline instruments: Check timer before heavy computations\n- Memory: 4 basis functions * integer resolution -> manageable\n- Behavior:\n    - Restart heavy if stuck in local optimum\n    - Track best solution found at each stage\n    - Maintain valid parent solution\n    - Ensure proper gradient calculation\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Ensure 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n- Enforce sum(h)*dx = 1 by interpolation/summing\n- Return best solution's C5 value computed properly\n- Verify solution meets all mathematical forms",
